8|0@GamerGhazi^1439828039#cu5zox4:m_data$:The vast majority of the games featured on Bain's list would either not benefit in any way from higher framerates, are technologically incapable of having a different framerate than they have or a different framerate would only produce a purely subjective difference. Also Bain's fanbase are known for viciously harassing anybody he points them toward a trait that he uses to great effect against people he considers his competitors or enemies. When he created that list he knew that every developer whose game appeared on it would as a result be buried under mountains of harassment like the developers of Guild of Dungeoneering were. It was an extremely unethical move for him to make. If somebody with a less hateful and irrational fanbase had done it and they had taken the care to only include games for which framerate clearly would matter and for which it is possible or desirable to have higher framerates nobody would have cared.
5|0@GamerGhazi^1439837558#cu65upt:EthicsOverwhelming$:The main reason is that this "policing" is done from a standpoint of ignorance. Bain, and almost assuredly no one else in the group has any experience with coding, programming or animating that would allow them to understand the mathematical or graphical reasons behind locking games at 30fps. I wish I had the source, but a self-proclaimed dev here (I think it was here anyways, and I only say self proclaimed because the person wasnt flashing around his work badge) went on a rant about how it was a stupid issue. Most people think "lazy devs" when they hear 30fps and don't understand that the decision for that was done so that other aspects of the game run smoothly (rendering, animating, coding, draw distance/pop-in etc etc) there are *valid, professional* reasons to do it, but the devs are being treated like pariahs and having their game put on some authoritarian watch list because of it. So the TL;DR version for me is that Bain is acting in arrogance by demanding games be held to his exacting standard as the Golden Standard and punishing them on this list of they don't, while also acting in total ignorance of any of the professional/developmental reasons they may have done it. 
